<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A BASE Info.plist, holding exactly one thing Xcode's build settings cannot express.

  `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE` stays YES: every other key — the bundle identifier, the supported
  orientations, the scene manifest, the five-language location purpose string — is still
  generated and merged on top of this file. This exists solely because the launch screen's
  BACKGROUND COLOUR is a nested key (`UILaunchScreen.UIColorName`) and the `INFOPLIST_KEY_*`
  bridge only covers flat ones: `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_Generation` is a real setting and
  `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_UIColorName` is not. Setting the latter looks entirely correct,
  builds without a warning, and is silently dropped — which is what happened here first, and
  what a screenshot of the launch screen caught.

  WHY IT IS WORTH A FILE. An empty `UILaunchScreen` dict is a BLANK WHITE screen, and it is
  shown for the whole of pre-main — measured at ~1.3 s on the simulator, which is 87% of this
  app's launch. On a phone in dark mode that is a full-screen white flash between the home
  screen and a dark app. `LaunchBackground` is the same colour the list draws on, in both
  appearances, so the launch screen looks like the app arriving rather than like a blank page.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
	</dict>
	<!--
	  The second thing here (2026-09-06): where the published store's manifest lives. This is
	  the ONE switch that turns the weekly refresh and the list's pull-to-check on; without it
	  the app never downloads a store and offers no pull. The URL is what
	  `.github/workflows/publish-store.yml` writes into `manifest.json` (`IOS_STORE_URL`), on
	  GitHub Pages. `https` only — `RefreshConfiguration.manifestURL` refuses anything else.
	-->
	<!-- Read by the About screen ("Made by"). Apple's own key, so it is also what Finder and
	     the App Store show; a NAME lives here, in data, and not as a literal in a Swift file. -->
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>© 2026 Gabriel Habryn</string>
	<key>SWIMZHStoreManifestURL</key>
	<string>https://widmogrod.github.io/swimming-in-zurich/manifest.json</string>
</dict>
</plist>
